ces each.\n\nLet\'s look at what would happen if Black played C4.\n\nHere is what the board would look like:\n\n```\n. . . * . . . .\n. . . * * . . .\n. . . * @ . . .\n```\n\nThe valid actions I have: (C, 3), (C, 5), (E, 3).\n\nSo the most effective move should be.\n\n1.  (C, 5): This will result in vertical lines between myself (White, @) and the opponent (Black, \\*).\n2.  (E, 3): This will result in a horizontal line of White and a triangle above (Black).\n3.  (C, 3): This results in a diagonal.\n\nSo my general strategy for picking the best opening move will revolve around the "perpendicular opening" and to avoid the Parallel Opening. With the position (E,3) being most effective.\n\nI think the best move here is to play (E, 3). This move has good structure:\n\n```\n. . . * . . . .\n. . . * * . . .\n. @ @ @ . . . .\n```\n\nWith E3, E4, and E5, I get a group of three. After this move, Black only has to move. If I played this move, the opponent is now constrained to 2 valid options (Black piece at the E column and black piece in F column).\n\nIn general, (E, 3) will be the most effective move, as there is high potential for a good strategic advantage.\n\n**Evaluation:** A solid opening move that minimizes opponent mobility.\n**Reason:** (E, 3) flips the piece at (E, 4), creating a stable horizontal line of pieces on row E. This avoids the disadvantageous parallel structures formed by (C, 5) and offers better control than the diagonal cut of (C, 3). It leaves the opponent with fewer good response options, adhering to the strategy of mobility maximization.\n**Answer:** (E, 3)\n\n\n', 'output': 'Evaluation: This is a solid opening move that prioritizes minimizing the opponent\'s mobility and creating a stable shape.\nReason: Placing a piece at (E, 3) captures the opponent\'s piece at (E, 4) horizontally, establishing a connected line of three friendly pieces on row E (E3, E4, E5). This formation is generally superior to the alternative (C, 5), which would create a "pa